220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 73 0 R
/T (A sofa mesh defines the shape of the object, but it does not automatically contain the fine grain associated with leather. Texture mapping places a two-dimensional image over the mesh by assigning UV coordinates to its vertices. The rasterizer interpolates these coordinates across each visible triangle, allowing the fragment shader to sample the appropriate colour from the albedo map.)
/E (A sofa mesh defines the shape of the object, but it does not automatically contain the fine grain associated with leather. Texture mapping places a two-dimensional image over the mesh by assigning UV coordinates to its vertices. The rasterizer interpolates these coordinates across each visible triangle, allowing the fragment shader to sample the appropriate colour from the albedo map.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 73 0 R
/T (4.5 Bump mapping and normal mapping)
/E (4.5 Bump mapping and normal mapping)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 73 0 R
/T (Bump mapping simulates small changes in surface orientation, while normal mapping stores those directions more directly. A tangent-space normal map stores x, y and z components in RGB channels. The shader converts the stored values from the [0,1] range into signed vector components, then uses a tangent-bitangent-normal \(TBN\) basis to transform the vector into world space. The resulting perturbed normal affects diffuse and specular shading, creating the appearance of pores and creases without changing the mesh boundaries.)
/E (Bump mapping simulates small changes in surface orientation, while normal mapping stores those directions more directly. A tangent-space normal map stores x, y and z components in RGB channels. The shader converts the stored values from the [0,1] range into signed vector components, then uses a tangent-bitangent-normal \(TBN\) basis to transform the vector into world space. The resulting perturbed normal affects diffuse and specular shading, creating the appearance of pores and creases without changing the mesh boundaries.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
