<<
/Title (Functional features include furniture rendering, selectable lighting modes, separate texture and bump switches, coordinate sliders, an inset-view option and keyboard/mouse input. The non-functional goals are clear presentation, predictable operation, low dependency overhead, reasonable rendering cost and reproducibility in the specified Unity version. A production WebXR application would also need device tracking, object placement, occlusion, camera permissions, performance profiling and accessibility testing; these are beyond the current desktop prototype.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 982 0 R
>>
endobj
993 0 obj
<<
/Title (A 3D mesh passes through a sequence of operations before appearing on screen. Its vertices are transformed from local space into world and camera space, projected, clipped and rasterized. During rasterization, attributes such as surface normals and UV coordinates are interpolated across triangles. Fragment shading then uses these values to calculate the final surface colour. The Unity example uses a compact forward-shading approach in ShaderLab/HLSL so that the lighting contributions can be inspected directly.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 984 0 R
>>
endobj
